{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,2]],"date-time":"2026-11-02T18:33:13Z","timestamp":1793644393796,"version":"4.5.1"},"reference-count":13,"publisher":"American Geophysical Union (AGU)","issue":"6","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2004,3,18]],"date-time":"2004-03-18T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1079568000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":17,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Geophysical Research Letters"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2004,3]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    The satellite CHAMP with its sensitive accelerometer on board provides the opportunity to investigate the thermospheric dynamics in great detail. In this study we concentrate on density structures in the cusp. During 25 Sep. 2000, the day we take as an example, air density enhancements of almost a factor of two are observed whenever the satellite passes the cusp region. For the interpretation of these events we consider also the concurrent ionospheric Hall and field\u2010aligned currents (FACs). As expected, sizable currents are found in the regions of dense air. Small\u2010scale FAC filaments (1\u2010km size) seem to play an important role in the heating. Whenever these very intense FACs with amplitudes of several hundreds of \u03bc\n                    <jats:italic>A<\/jats:italic>\n                    \/\n                    <jats:italic>m<\/jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n                    show up, density enhancements occur.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1029\/2003gl019314","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2004,3,17]],"date-time":"2004-03-17T16:00:56Z","timestamp":1079539256000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":204,"title":["Thermospheric up\u2010welling in the cusp region: Evidence from CHAMP observations"],"